  "source": {
    "full": "Stefanie Ramos Bierge, Clause Types and Transitivity in Wixárika (Huichol): A Uto-Aztecan Language (PhD dissertation, University of Colorado, 2016); José Luis Iturrioz Leza, Paula Gómez López and Xitakame Ramírez de la Cruz, «La morfología verbal», in Lenguas y literaturas indígenas de Jalisco (Secretaría de Cultura de Jalisco). Spelling of Iturrioz et al. (1999). See also: Joseph E. Grimes et al., El huichol: apuntes sobre el léxico (Cornell University, 1981)."
  },
  "about": [
    "Wixárika, or Huichol, is a language of the Uto-Aztecan family, closely related to Náyeri (Cora). It is spoken in the Sierra Madre Occidental, in northern Jalisco and parts of Nayarit, Durango and Zacatecas, by about 60,000 people.",
    "Its speakers call themselves wixaritari (singular wixárika) and their language wixárika or wixaritari niukiyari. The main communities are San Andrés Cohamiata (Tateikie), Santa Catarina (Tuapuritari) and San Sebastián (Wautia). Every year they make a pilgrimage to Wirikuta, in San Luis Potosí.",
    "It is a highly polysynthetic language: a single verb can gather more than twenty pieces, saying who does it, to whom, where, how, how many times and with what certainty. Many verbs change root with the number of the subject or when the action is finished: mie «goes», hu «they go», tɨa «went», kɨ «they went»."
  ],
  "orthography": {
    "intro": "We use the spelling of Iturrioz and colleagues (1999): 5 vowels, which can be long (written double), and 13 consonants. Ɨ is a vowel between i and u; x sounds like a retroflex r, and the apostrophe (') is the glottal stop.",
