    "notes": [
      "Length changes the meaning: haari «crocodile», hari «gourd»; tuutú «flower», tutu «ancestor».",
      "Stress also tells words apart: táame «we», taamé «tooth». It is only written when it doesn't fall on the second-to-last syllable: 'ukí «man», maxa «deer».",
      "There is no o in Wixárika: u can sound like o (teyupani sounds like «teyopani»).",
      "Pronunciation is shown in brackets with the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA)."
    ]
  },
  "grammar": {
    "intro": "A Wixárika verb puts almost everything before the root: who does it, the speaker's certainty, to whom, where and how many times. After the root come tense and aspect.",
